From exploring the nx (TypeScript) speed residual. ### The bug `_var_decl_pattern` = `^[ 	]*(?:[^=
]{0,80}[ 	]+)?([A-Za-z_]\w{0,63})[ 	]*=(?!=)` (re.M) backtracked its greedy `[^=
]{0,80}` prefix across **every line** before failing — but it can **never** match a line without a bare `=` (the pattern requires one, and `[^=
]` can't cross a newline). Profiling put this at ~a quarter of `splice()` on large TS files. ### The fix Iterate lines, skip those without `=` (cheap membership test). `.match` per line is identical to the original re.M `^`-anchored `finditer` (anchors at line start, ≤1 match/line, in line order), so every `equations` count is byte-for-byte unchanged. ### Parity (behavior unchanged) - Identifier lists **identical over 800 real TS/JS files** (0 mismatches). - **Both golden-crucible legs pass** (full-precision + zero-dependency) — corpus-wide extraction output unchanged across all languages. - 8,124 extraction tests, ruff, mypy all green. ### Impact — and an honest caveat - **8.2× on the var-decl pass**, ~15% on a 561 KB TS file. - **But** a whole-repo scan's `PASS_1` is parallel across cores, so nx's wall-time is parallelism-bound and barely moves (77.1 s → 76.4 s). nx's 4× residual is **structural** (heavy TS per-LOC work across the brace-slicer + coding-rules + var-decl passes), not a single hot loop. - Shipped as a CPU/backtracking-efficiency fix: it removes real wasted work across the whole 280 M-LOC corpus and helps any single-threaded path, even t
